 ## Background
-Gaussian Piling (GaussianPile) was introduced to bridge the gap between the high compression ratio of Implicit Neural Representations (INRs) and the fast fitting of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) for slice-based volumetric imaging [S1, unofficial]. Standard 3DGS is designed for surface appearance from multi-view images and discards internal volumetric information, making it unsuitable for scientific and medical imaging [S1, unofficial]. GaussianPile re-engineers the core rendering principle of Gaussian primitives by incorporating the physical properties of the imaging system, specifically the point spread function (PSF) [S1, unofficial]. It was evaluated on microscopy and ultrasound datasets, demonstrating superior performance in modeling internal structures compared to NeRF-based approaches and standard 3DGS [S1, unofficial].
+Gaussian Piling (GaussianPile) was introduced to bridge the gap between the high compression ratio of Implicit Neural Representations (INRs) and the fast fitting of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) for slice-based volumetric imaging [S1, unofficial]. Standard 3DGS is fundamentally designed to model surface appearance from multi-view images and discards internal volumetric information, making it unsuitable for scientific and medical imaging where complete internal structure is paramount [S1, unofficial]. The method unifies 3D Gaussian splatting with an imaging system-aware focus model, incorporating the physical properties of the imaging system (specifically the point spread function) into the rendering pipeline [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented in PyTorch and CUDA, trained with the Adam optimizer for 30k iterations [S1, unofficial].
 
